# Line-ending policy.
#
# This repository is developed on Windows and built on Linux (GitHub Actions,
# ubuntu-latest). A contributor with `core.autocrlf=true` — the Windows default
# — would otherwise commit CRLF line endings, and that breaks two things that
# are easy to miss until CI fails:
#
#   1. `gradlew` is a POSIX shell script. With CRLF it fails on Linux with
#      `bad interpreter: /bin/sh^M`, and the error names the interpreter rather
#      than the line endings, so it reads as a missing-file problem.
#   2. `tools/kotlin_lint.py` and `tools/kotlin_refcheck.py` are invoked as
#      `python tools/<x>.py`. Same failure mode, and they run as the FIRST CI
#      steps, so a CRLF checkout would turn a source-hygiene check into a
#      confusing crash before the build even starts.
#
# Everything a Linux process executes or parses must therefore be LF. Text that
# is only ever read by Windows tooling can stay native, but there is nothing
# here that qualifies, so the default is `text=auto` and the exceptions are
# explicit.

# Default: let Git normalise, storing LF in the repository.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Shell scripts must be LF in the working tree too, not just in the object
# store. `eol=lf` is what enforces that on checkout.
gradlew text eol=lf
*.sh    text eol=lf

# Python is executed by the CI runner, so it must be LF as well.
*.py    text eol=lf

# Kotlin sources: compiled by the Linux runner's Kotlin compiler. Kotlin is
# line-ending agnostic, but keeping them LF means a diff produced on Windows and
# a diff produced on Linux are identical, which keeps review noise down.
*.kt    text eol=lf

# Build and resource files that are read by Gradle on the Linux runner.
*.kts   text eol=lf
*.toml  text eol=lf
*.xml   text eol=lf
*.json  text eol=lf
*.pro   text eol=lf
*.md    text eol=lf

# Windows batch scripts genuinely need CRLF — cmd.exe mishandles LF in some
# constructs (notably labels and multi-line parenthesised blocks).
*.bat   text eol=crlf

# Binary assets must never be touched by any normalisation. Without this, a
# `text=auto` rule can still decide a file is text if it happens to contain no
# NUL bytes, and re-encoding a TrueType font or a JKS keystore corrupts it
# irreversibly — the corruption is silent until a build fails to load the font
# or the signing step rejects the key.
*.ttf        binary
*.otf        binary
*.keystore   binary
*.jks        binary
*.png        binary
*.jpg        binary
*.jpeg       binary
*.webp       binary
*.zip        binary
*.jar        binary
*.apk        binary
*.aab        binary

# Gradle wrapper jars are binaries and are already covered by *.jar above, but
# named explicitly because a corrupted wrapper is a supply-chain problem rather
# than a build annoyance.
gradle/wrapper/gradle-wrapper.jar binary

# Keep generated artifacts out of `git diff` entirely.
app/build/**   -diff
build/**       -diff
.gradle/**     -diff
